n at a at  nine percent at the moment they're gonna  have to pay more for another holiday so  what can the government do to help well  this is why we want to work really  closely with the airports andy and the  um the airlines as well to make sure  that they are doing everything they can  and and see what more we can do from uh  organizations of the department for  working pensions our work coaches to  make sure that in a tight labor market  where people um can work more hours if  they if they're able to uh and really  matching up so that's the answer is that  people have to just work hard  well  what i mean is that the uh there are a  record number of vacancies 1.3 million  vacancies across the country in all  manner of sectors but um there are also  people who have recalibrated what they  want to do when they're on furlough and  we want to make sure that those people  that are not necessarily working  full-time um  through universal credit we can we can  get them back in to to work to be more  productive if that if that suits them  and obviously match them up with it with  the sectors where there are with those  vacancies okay it sounds like you are  saying you want people to work longer  hours and well  we're not talking about going out and  forcing people to do anything but they  just want to make sure that they're  matched up properly so that it's just  got  that those people who can work longer  that want to work longer can work can be  better matched with the work coaches and  work closely with their work coaches how  bad is this going to get we saw the bank  of england raising interest rates for  the fifth consecutive month a warning of  more rises ahead inflation they say  could go up to 11  how bad is the situation are we heading  for a recession well i think what you  what we are saying we do we're trying to  do everything we can to tackle the cost  of living uh issues this is a global  situation you've seen inflation in other  countries in america and ot