l standards well i think my  understanding of what i read that when i  read the letter was that that was  talking about uh advice that he'd asked  him to give not not about not about the  impossible  yeah which which i can't really  reconcile my myself because that was  about advice he he's asked him on  something else rather than uh what's  happening around around partygate but in  in terms of the the fine the prime  minister received uh sugray talked about  leadership uh within number 10 and the  cabinet office and there's been a raft  of changes in number 10 and the cabinet  office in terms of organization in terms  of changing that culture changing that  ethos and sugue herself said that she  wants that that needs time to bed in law  guy was clearly very frustrated with the  whole way that that was handled as well  so will there be another ethics advisor  of that i don't know i mean there's a  there's a number of questions that uh  lord guy has has raised about the way  that uh the the way that that system  works and that's right that number 10  actually takes time to just um look at  that and work work that through so um  that'd be for them to announce would  anyone want to do the job it looks like  anyone who does the job is overruled by  the prime minister no well it's as i say  that i think that's that's part of the  uh the the  the consideration is is is how the  system works and uh because we want to  make it um as efficient uh  you know working as as best we can and  um that's what the prime minister number  10 will consider would you be  comfortable if there was no ethics  advisor what kind of message do you  think that would send down what what i  think i would be comfortable with as  long as there is a mechanism there needs  to be a mechanism clearly that the the  prime minister and that me as a minister  are held to the to the highest standards  there is a ministerial code there and we  want to make sure that um that it's  adhered to because that's the principles  that we all stand on not just as mps  when we first come into the house but  when we accept officers of state as  ministers do you think they should go to  parliament do you think this is  something that mps more broadly should  decide on and make sure that there's a  truly independent ethics advisor to keep  the prime minister and the government in  check i think the you know it's  difficult because you then get into  positions where um you know government  government governs and parliament  scrutinizes that parliament would  undoubtedly continue to debate this uh  this matter but i'm not sure that uh  that that day-to-day running of  government  you know the government is beholden to  parliament in that sort of slightly in  in that balance of power as it were  that's so in our unwritten constitution  i'm not sure that's how it works we've  had an extraordinary week in politics  this week haven't we and we've had uh  the bill on the northern ireland  protocol we've had the rwanda policy  seeing uh flights uh grounded  by a court in  in europe  does the prime minister think he can  breach whichever rules he wants and get  away with it because the european  commission vice president uh told our  political editor  that constant breaching of international  law by the country is damaging uk eu  relationships  that i'm afraid just a repeat of sort of  negotiating in public that we've seen  over the last few years i mean i don't  believe uh and the government's been  really clear that uh the legislation  that we've introduced doesn't reach  international law but nonetheless  actually it's there as an insurance  policy anyway what would rather have is  a negotiated settlement with the eu we  don't want to end up in trade wars and  and escalated disputes we want to make  sure that we can work together because  this affects both sides and so if we can  have sensible conversations  um rather than as i say negotiating via  the media or via social media and these 